  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Holwaya mucida is a rare and understudied plant species endemic to New England, belonging to the monotypic family Holwayaceae. This small herbaceous plant is notable for its mucilaginous (slimy) characteristics, which give it its scientific epithet 'mucida'.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Mucilaginous Texture",
      "description": "The plant's stems and leaves have a distinctly slimy or gelatinous coating when touched, which is the defining characteristic of the species.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Stature",
      "description": "Holwaya mucida is a diminutive plant, typically reaching only a few inches in height with correspondingly small leaves and flowers.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Unique Family Characteristics",
      "description": "As the only known member of family Holwayaceae in North America, it displays distinctive floral and leaf morphology unlike other regional plants.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species thrives in moist, shaded woodland understory environments and wet seepage areas in northern New England, preferring acidic soils near streams or spring-fed areas.",
